Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:15:An integrally closed ring `R` contains all the elements of `Frac(R)` that are
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:16:integral over `R`. A special case of integrally closed rings are the Dedekind domains.
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:20:* `IsIntegrallyClosedIn R A` states `R` contains all integral elements of `A`
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:21:* `IsIntegrallyClosed R` states `R` contains all integral elements of `Frac(R)`
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:26:  is integrally closed iff it is the integral closure of `R` in `K`
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:32:A *normal domain* is a domain that is integrally closed in its field of fractions.
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:34:Normal domains are the major use case of `IsIntegrallyClosed` at the time of writing, and we have
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:35:quite a few results that can be moved wholesale to a new `NormalDomain` definition.
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:36:In fact, before PR https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/6126 `IsIntegrallyClosed` was exactly defined to be a normal domain.
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:41:This implies `IsIntegrallyClosed`,
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:51:`IsIntegrallyClosed` or `NormalDomain`. If we use `NormalDomain` then `IsReduced` is automatic,
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:52:but we could also consider a version of `NormalDomain` that only requires the localizations are
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:53:`IsIntegrallyClosed` but may not be domains, and that may not equivalent to the ring itself being
Mathlib/RingTheory/IntegralClosure/IntegrallyClosed.lean:54:`IsIntegrallyClosed` (even for Noetherian rings?).
